<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Motion Estimation on Mi&amp;Bee Blog</title><link>https://blog.mickeyzzc.tech/en/tags/motion-estimation/</link><description>Recent content in Motion Estimation on Mi&amp;Bee Blog</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en</language><managingEditor>蓝宝石的傻话</managingEditor><lastBuildDate>Mon, 24 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://blog.mickeyzzc.tech/en/tags/motion-estimation/rss.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Why Video Compresses 100×: Redundancy, YUV, I/P/B Frames and GOP</title><link>https://blog.mickeyzzc.tech/en/posts/network/video-compression-fundamentals/</link><pubDate>Mon, 24 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.mickeyzzc.tech/en/posts/network/video-compression-fundamentals/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;The later posts on &lt;a href="../web-video-codec-survey/"&gt;browser codec selection&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="../streaming-protocols-survey/"&gt;streaming protocol panorama&lt;/a&gt; keep leaning on H.264, I-frames and GOP — this post fills in the foundation first: what actually happens when a stream of pixels turns into a few Mbps. No math derivations — mechanisms, each tied to its engineering consequence (latency, seeking, corruption, storage).&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Start with some arithmetic.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="how-big-is-uncompressed-video"&gt;How big is uncompressed video&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;One 1080p (1920×1080), 30fps, 8bit RGB stream:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="code-block-wrapper" data-lang="text"&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="code-block-header"&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="code-block-meta"&gt;&lt;span class="code-language"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#10; &#10; &lt;button class="copy-button" aria-label="Copy code"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="copy-icon" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"&gt;&lt;rect x="9" y="9" width="13" height="13" rx="2" ry="2"/&gt;&lt;path d="M5 15H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="check-icon" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"&gt;&lt;polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/button&gt;&#10; &#10; &lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="code-block-body"&gt;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;&#10;&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;&#10;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;2&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;3&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;4&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;&#10;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-text" data-lang="text"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;pixels per frame = 1920 × 1080 = 2,073,600&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;bytes per frame = 2,073,600 × 3 (RGB) ≈ 5.93 MB&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;per second = 5.93 MB × 30 ≈ 178 MB/s&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;bitrate ≈ 1.49 Gbps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;That is 10.7 GB per minute. 4K (3840×2160) multiplies by another 4: ~42.7 GB/min. Sixteen surveillance cameras recording for a month at these rates is beyond any storage system. In practice 1080p surveillance streams run 2–8 Mbps — a compression ratio on the order of several hundred to one. Compression is not an optimization; it is the precondition for video existing at all.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>